miniramfs — Mini RAM-Based File System demo   (kernel 6.8.12-fuse2005)

━━━ [1] Load module ━━━
$ insmod src/miniramfs.ko
$ grep miniramfs /proc/filesystems
nodev	miniramfs

━━━ [2] Operation 1: MOUNT ━━━
$ mount -t miniramfs none /mnt/test
$ mount | grep miniramfs
none on /mnt/test type miniramfs (rw,relatime)

━━━ [3] Operation 2: CREATE DIRECTORY (mkdir) ━━━
$ mkdir /mnt/test/docs /mnt/test/docs/sub

━━━ [4] Operation 3: CREATE FILE ━━━
$ touch /mnt/test/docs/empty.txt
$ ls -l /mnt/test/docs/empty.txt
-rw-r--r-- 1 root root 0 Nov 25 14:27 /mnt/test/docs/empty.txt
# new empty file: size 0

━━━ [5] Operation 4: WRITE FILE ━━━
$ echo 'CPE333 OS' > /mnt/test/docs/note.txt
$ echo 'Line 2' >> /mnt/test/docs/note.txt
# append: size grows from 10 to 17 bytes

━━━ [6] Operation 5: READ FILE ━━━
$ cat /mnt/test/docs/note.txt
CPE333 OS
Line 2

━━━ [7] Operation 6: LIST DIRECTORY (ls) ━━━
$ ls -la /mnt/test
total 4
drwxr-xr-x 3 root root    0 Nov 25 14:27 .
drwxr-xr-x 8 root root 4096 Nov 24 22:12 ..
drwxr-xr-x 3 root root    0 Nov 25 14:27 docs
$ ls -la /mnt/test/docs
total 0
drwxr-xr-x 3 root root  0 Nov 25 14:27 .
drwxr-xr-x 3 root root  0 Nov 25 14:27 ..
-rw-r--r-- 1 root root  0 Nov 25 14:27 empty.txt
-rw-r--r-- 1 root root 17 Nov 25 14:27 note.txt
drwxr-xr-x 2 root root  0 Nov 25 14:27 sub

━━━ [8] Limit check: write > 1 MiB is rejected (EFBIG), no crash ━━━
$ head -c 2M /dev/zero > /mnt/test/big.bin
head: error writing 'standard output': File too large
$ ls -l /mnt/test/big.bin
-rw-r--r-- 1 root root 1048576 Nov 25 14:27 /mnt/test/big.bin

━━━ [9] Operation 7: UNMOUNT + remove module ━━━
$ umount /mnt/test
$ ls /mnt/test
# data lived only in RAM -> gone after umount
$ rmmod miniramfs

━━━ [10] Kernel log (dmesg) ━━━
$ dmesg | grep 'miniramfs:' | tail -5
[  358.114897] miniramfs: module unloaded (live files=0, live bytes=0)
[  373.509867] miniramfs: module loaded
[  374.510647] miniramfs: mounted
[  381.374663] miniramfs: unmounted (live files=0, live bytes=0)
[  381.432489] miniramfs: module unloaded (live files=0, live bytes=0)
# live files=0, live bytes=0  -> every buffer was freed (no leak)

Demo finished.
